The title and description inputs must be visibly open before the extractor
returns set metadata. The read-only `Edit Info` opener checks hidden form
identity even when the form has not become visible, then opens it only once.
Worker retries respect that opener's boolean result and refuse to read after
a rejected opener; no Save/submit action is invoked. Synthetic DOM and worker
regressions cover wrong/duplicate form IDs and hidden metadata controls.

The extractor and opener now scope visible metadata selectors to that same
unique form, rejecting missing or duplicate controls. Unrelated title,
description, or privacy controls elsewhere in the document cannot be returned
as evidence for the requested set.

The read-only Edit Info opener now independently checks the same authenticated
shell and visible human-action blockers as the detail extractor. Organization
selection, a visible CAPTCHA, a password overlay, or a missing logout control
stops the opener without clicking Edit Info. This protects the interval between
session observation and opening metadata controls, not remote atomicity.

The extension's set-detail worker now retries only an exact page-unavailable
reply while the Edit Info panel hydrates. A malformed success envelope or
unexpected opener result is rejected immediately rather than accepted after
a later retry. Before returning metadata, the worker rechecks the exact edit
URL and requires two identical observations separated by an event-loop yield.

Synthetic worker tests reject tab switches and changes to the visible set title
between those observations. These checks do not establish a remote atomic
snapshot and never submit the set metadata form.

This adds no claim of live provider acceptance or server-side atomicity.

Recovered My Sets module 52644 and its captured route HTML establish the true
empty-library state as "You'll need a question set to host!" plus the visible
"Create a Set" action. The adapter returns an empty set list only with both
markers; zero cards alone, folder-empty text, and search-empty text still fail
closed so loading cannot masquerade as an empty account.

The canonical `sets.list` URL strips search/folder query state before
reading.
The set summary and detail readers reject duplicate `id` query parameters,
even if each duplicate has the same value. The canonical set-list decoder
also bounds observed lists to the existing 200-card local browser safety cap
before item traversal; neither check claims a provider collection-size limit.

The visible `Edit Info` opener now verifies the exact expected set ID before
clicking or accepting an already-open panel. A switched edit route cannot
trigger even this presentation-only action for another set.

The canonical set-ID, summary, and detail decoders now enforce the same
local 512-character opaque ID, 1,000-character nonblank title, and
10,000-character description limits as the browser extractors.
Oversized or NUL-bearing IDs fail before application session access. These
are not provider contract limits.

Set ID admission is also aligned across CLI, canonical read ports, browser
bridge, and page extraction: control characters (including newline, tab, DEL)
are not valid opaque IDs. This rejects transport-dependent interpretations
without inventing a provider ID grammar.

The read-only capability inspection host now enforces the same control-byte
exclusion on an untrusted candidate set ID before navigating to its edit page.
It also requires an exact two-field successful set-list envelope; unexpected
outer fields and malformed IDs fail without opening Add Question. Synthetic
probes verify that the original tab URL is restored on those failures.
The same admission guards run inside the injected Add Question, capability, and
question-panel functions before opening or interacting with visible controls.

Because the page boundary can emit an empty array only for that exact recovered
