The authenticated capability inspection application likewise checks the
exact probe result envelope before decoding any account evidence. Unknown
failure codes and unexpected fields become stable browser failures instead
of a successful capability result or a leaked diagnostic.

The local bridge adapter independently checks the exact transport response
envelope before returning session, capability, set, question, or write data.
Invalid runtime success and failure shapes become a stable browser failure;
no raw transport properties or diagnostic strings leave that boundary.

Canonical set and question read applications also admit only exact browser
probe envelopes. Malformed failure codes, extra fields on success or failure,
and invalid runtime shapes become stable secret-free read failures before
reaching the public command layer; decoded content remains independently gated.

Session observation and one-shot authentication now validate the browser port's
exact runtime result shape, not merely its TypeScript declaration. Unexpected
fields, synthetic success flags, and unknown failure codes become a stable
secret-free browser failure; only a fresh observed ready page grants access.

Portable browser authentication is now implemented fail-closed. Question
listing remains a supported observational read: the worker navigates to the
exact set edit route, bounds the visible Edit-question controls, opens each
question, decodes the observed serialized question and normalized answer facts,
and verifies panel cleanup.

The Chrome question-read worker now runs a deadline-bounded, non-mutating
inspection host. It checks each modal's parsed question through the canonical
version-three decoder, cancels and confirms panel closure, and re-enumerates
the exact visible question numbers before publishing the result. Any insertion,
deletion, reorder, tab switch, malformed script result, or deadline exhaustion
fails the entire read instead of returning a mixed visible collection. The
eight-second scan budget includes edit-route navigation and leaves a margin
before the bridge's ten-second request timeout.

The modal inspection host now distinguishes one exact page-unavailable reply
from malformed success data. Only the former can be retried while React
hydrates.
A mismatched question number, unexpected field, or invalid success envelope
fails the whole read on the first such observation, even if a later observation
would be valid. Cancellation and closed-panel confirmation still run once after
the malformed read; they are not skipped to preserve a partial collection.

The normalized collection also has a cumulative 750 KB UTF-8 JSON budget
below the localhost bridge's 1 MB request-body cap. Large but individually
valid questions therefore fail before an oversized bridge reply is emitted.

The extension now tolerates client hydration after the tab's document reaches
`complete`: it retries only the exact known page-level unavailable reply up to
15 times at 100 ms intervals inside the shared eight-second read deadline.
Malformed envelopes, duplicate numbers, and unexpected values still fail at
once without starting an edit. After a visible empty list, it yields once to
the event loop before re-enumerating the controls, rejecting any list drift.
This reduces transient loading failures without proving atomic server state,
remote list completeness, or a fully hydrated empty-account response.

Synthetic tests verify the normal path, drift, exception cleanup, expiration,
